WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.840
Let’s talk about the Central African Republic.

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:11.720
The fighting there is so bad that more than 200,000 people have fled their homes since December.

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:26.920
There’s a tug of war between two men — the president who just got himself re-elected in a messy vote and a former president who's under UN sanctions and wants his old job back.

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:38.880
So why did an election stir things up? Who’s fighting who in the CAR? And why are the Russians in there?

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:52.480
The Central African Republic is a former French colony that’s basically the same size as France.

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:57.840
But where France has almost 70 million people, the CAR has less than five million.

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:04.000
And while it has a lot of natural resources, the average person lives on less than $2 a day.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:10.160
Things were bad under colonial rule but independence in the CAR has also been a rough ride.

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:15.440
The man meant to become the first president died in a dodgy plane crash.

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:23.840
In the ’70s the CAR had a brutal leader who declared himself president for life, then emperor, before the French got rid of him.

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:31.080
There have been coups, two civil wars and a handful of disappointing peace deals.

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:47.960
So 60 years later the CAR is pretty much run by whoever has the most guns.

00:01:47.960 --> 00:01:54.240
There are maybe 20 militias who control two-thirds of the country and do pretty much whatever they like.

00:01:54.240 --> 00:02:01.760
Some of them started out with some kind of cause but many resorted to killing, extortion and fighting over resources.

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:10.960
Now the main rivalry is often laid out as being between the Seleka, which is mostly made up of Muslim groups, and the Anti-balaka, who are mostly Christian fighters.

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:18.680
But it’s not as simple as Muslims versus Christians. There are all kinds of shifting alliances and other local disputes in the mix.

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:39.040
OK, to understand the fighting going on now we have to get into the fight between these two: President Faustin-Archange Touadera and former president Francois Bozize.

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:43.680
At one point they were in the same government — Touadera was Bozize’s prime minister.

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:46.640
So Bozize took power in a coup in 2003.

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:51.240
And he’s been accused of having a hand in all sorts of atrocities, which he denies. 

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:56.680
Fast forward 10 years and Bozize’s overthrown by Seleka rebels. And he leaves the country.

00:02:56.680 --> 00:03:00.600
What followed were two years of conflict and chaos. 

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:14.480
The Seleka struggled to keep control. An alliance known as Anti-balaka emerged to fight the Seleka. It was made up of many Christian militias.

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:31.360
Eventually France’s military stepped in and there was a peace deal between the government and some of the militias.

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:34.560
They called elections and Touadera ended up winning.

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:45.640
So in 2016 Touadera’s the president. Meanwhile Bozize’s in exile and he’s been accused of supporting Anti-balaka rebels, which is why the UN imposed sanctions on him.

00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:49.240
Now while Touadera was in office things did calm down a bit.

00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:55.000
But militias still controlled large parts of the country and the peace deal ended up falling apart.

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:59.720
So in 2019 the government signed a new deal with 14 armed groups.

00:03:59.720 --> 00:04:04.640
The plan was to integrate some militia leaders into the government and fighters into the army.

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:09.480
Things were looking up and the elections last year were supposed to mark a new start.

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:11.840
And then Bozize came back on the scene. 

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:21.520
He returned to the CAR and wanted to run for president, but the Constitutional Court banned him, saying he failed to meet the standards of “good morality”.

00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:44.840
This time some Seleka and Anti-balaka factions put their differences aside and formed a new alliance called the Coalition of Patriots for Change.

00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:54.240
Many say Bozize is leading the rebels though he denies that.

00:05:05.080 --> 00:05:08.880
Now all of that happened before the elections in December.

00:05:09.560 --> 00:05:16.680
So when it came time to vote many people, especially in rebel-controlled areas, either couldn’t or were too scared to.

00:05:19.480 --> 00:05:30.160
But in the end Touadera was re-elected president. And even though opposition groups accused him of fraud, the Constitutional Court ruled that he won in the first round.

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:51.960
You can imagine how Touadera’s enemies reacted. The CPC, that coalition of militias, stepped up their war. And people have been trying to escape.

00:06:06.760 --> 00:06:09.680
They’re running from murder, rape and kidnapping.

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:21.960
Things are now so much worse in a country where a quarter of the population was already displaced.

00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:28.880
Right now the government is fighting a huge group of heavily armed and well-organised militias.

00:06:28.880 --> 00:06:42.960
So it’s relying on outside help to turn things around: 300 French soldiers, 11,000 UN peacekeeping troops, the Rwandan army’s in there. Russia too — they say, to train the CAR army.

00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:59.400
So a whole lot of outsiders, each with their own interests.

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:02.560
The people of the CAR, though, have other concerns.

00:07:14.520 --> 00:07:20.320
People reckon the best way forward is to somehow revive that peace deal from 2019.

00:07:20.320 --> 00:07:25.360
To do that the president, the former president and all their allies need to come together.

00:07:25.360 --> 00:07:30.320
But that won’t be easy in a country with a lot of players — and too many guns.

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:41.760
Al Jazeera is always monitoring what’s happening in the CAR so check our website for the latest and YouTube for reports from our correspondents on the ground.

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:42.760
I’ll see you next week.

